// Review -- Introduction //
Transistor is a futuristic 2-D brawler with limited, but well-implemented, turn-based tactical elements.   It has excellent music, excellent hand-drawn art, and excellent combat gameplay; an excellent story, and an excellent narration.   Developed by Supergiant Games, the creators of Bastion.
// Music //
I start here first, because the music sold me on the game.  Ashley Barrett is easily a grade A vocalist, and her work with Darren Korb on the original sountrack makes for an incredible, unique, and atmospheric musical score.  The game sports a wonderful feature; not only are all the songs accessible in-game via the 'Backdoor' space, acting as a sort of lair of easter eggs; but, further, pressing the LB button on the X-Input layout (and whatever it is on keyboard) will cause Red (voiced by Ashley Barrett) to hum in sync with the ambient background music.  These humming tracks can be found on the Extended Soundtrack; and, are quite wonderful.
// Art //
I mention this second because, this sold me on another two copies of this game. xD The music and art are impeccable, the visual art is hand-drawn and as beautiful, if not more, than Bastion's.  It fits the digital, futuristic setting; rather, it is that setting.  It brings it to life.  The music colors it in. xD
// Gameplay -- Combat //
The combat reminds me of Bastion, but the mechanics are somewhat different.  It remains a 2D Top-down isometric brawler, but instead of multiple weapons you now have multiple abilities.   You can equip up to four active abilities at a time; any abilities left over can be equipped to these four actives as passive abilities that alter the active ability's function.  For instance, making a short-range magical cleaving attack a long range attack, or giving it multiple cones of damage, or giving it an explosive radius, etc.  It adds a surprisingly deep and rewarding amount of customization to the game's otherwise rather simplistic mechanics.
// Gameplay -- Story //
A black screen fades to the first scene, Red is holding Transistor as he impales his first victim.  First minute, guess what? Your only companion is a transhuman talking circuit board broadsword!  Yes'm, you summon digital magic with your transhuman friend.  Now, as for your magic, remember those fellow citizens who shared Cloudbank City with you?  Well...time to consume the remnants of their being and use them as powers to combat the 'Process'! (A robotic extermination of Cloudbank City's residents)  The conceptualization, presentation, and execution of the story all seem to be quite well-thought out and original, and leave me feeling refreshed.
// Conclusion //
Transistor is an original take on an age-old genre.  More than worth the 20$.  The soundtrack is exceptional, and worth the additional money, in my opinion.

